==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|aspersus}}, past participle of {{m|la|aspergere}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈspɜːs/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-asperse.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈspɝs/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)s|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[sprinkle]] or [[scatter]] (liquid or dust).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Derek Walcott|title=The Prodigal|publisher=Farrar, Straus, Giroux|page=102|passage=a hand in San Marco's font / '''aspersed''' him with foul canal water}}
# To [[false]]ly or [[malicious]]ly [[charge]] someone.
#: {{syn|en|slander}}
#* {{RQ:Tatler|pseudonym=Bickerstaff|author=Addison and Steele|issue=160|date=18 April 1710|page=409|passage=It hath always been my way to baffle reproach with silence; though I cannot but observe the disingenuous proceedings of this gentleman, who is not content to '''asperse''' my writings, but hath wounded, through my sides, those eminent and worthy citizens, Mr. {{w|John Morphew}}, and Mr. [[w:Barnaby Bernard Lintot|Bernard Lintot]].}}
#* '''1749''', {{w|Henry Fielding}}, ''{{w|The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling}},'' Dublin: John Smith, Volume{{nbsp}}2, Book{{nbsp}}8, Chapter{{nbsp}}8, pp.{{nbsp}}126-127,<sup>[https://name.umdl.umich.edu/004794856.0001.002]</sup>
#*: This is indeed a most aggravating Circumstance, which attends unjustly depriving Men of their Reputation; for a Man who is Conscious of having an ill Character, cannot justly be angry with those who neglect and slight him; but ought rather to despise those that affect his Conversation, unless where a perfect Intimacy must have convinced them that their Friend’s Character hath been falsely and injuriously '''aspersed'''.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1844|author={{w|Charles Dickens}}|title={{w|Martin Chuzzlewit}}|location=London|publisher=Chapman and Hall|chapter=32|page=394|url=https://archive.org/details/adventuresoflife00dickrich/page/394/mode/1up?q=aspersed
|passage=If any one had taxed him with the vice, he would have indignantly repelled the accusation, and conceived himself unworthily '''aspersed'''.}}

